Z||000000||BAM-BAM||04-09-2001||08:28 PM|||| I just ordered KMC-782's throught Discount tire,17x7 +40. I was on the Discount Direct website ( <A HREF="http://www.discounttiredirect.com" TARGET=_blank>www.discounttiredirect.com</A>  ) and looked up compatable tire sizes. I also did a Forum search for applicable tire sizes. DTD recommended either 215/40-17 or 235/40-17. Is there a problem with the 235/40-17 on a 7" wheel? In all the posts I read there was concern that it wasn't a proper fit. I want to go as fat as I can on a 7" wheel. Can you give me some advise? I would greatly appriciate it. TIA<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/KMC782.JPG"> <P>Peace, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Knowledge is POWER!<BR>That is why the ignorant remain Weak!||||reg||1
Z||000001||fiero56||04-09-2001||09:47 PM||||Bam,<P>I'm just going to say 235 is wide for a 7" rim. Stock on 7" rims is 215 and I feel that is too wide for most "performace" handling. For autocrossing, etc you want 85-90 percent of the tire supported by the rim, which is right around 205. I don't think it is possible to put such a wide tire on that rim becuase there is very little sidewall to "absorb" the difference. I'm no expert, but I don't think it will work.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Slammed Fiero||04-09-2001||10:44 PM|||| <A HREF="http://powerdog.com/tiresize.cgi" TARGET=_blank>http://powerdog.com/tiresize.cgi</A> <P>This always helps me Big time in calculating sizes. It gives differneces in percentage between difference size tires ,<P><BR>A great tool <P>JM ||||reg||1
Z||000003||85Josh||04-09-2001||10:56 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.secret-secret.com/turbo/DIY/tirecalc/turbotire.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.secret-secret.com/turbo/DIY/tirecalc/turbotire.html</A> <BR>This site shows you the difference between two sizes.<P>------------------<BR>85 2M4(Lil' Bit)||||reg||1
Z||000004||BAM-BAM||04-09-2001||10:59 PM|||| Does it make a difference that I don't indulge myself in extreme handling situations? I am the laidback daily driver who enjoys those momentary bursts.I won't be taking my new and improved Fiero to the track (well maybe once or twice)I just want to get into the FLOW. I respect all of your inputs and thank you very much for your time.  Tomorrow will tell, that's when the wheels will be in and we tryout the different fitments. I'll be asking alot of questions and will definately relay the answers. Thanks again.<P>Peace, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <P>------------------<BR>Knowledge is POWER!<BR>That is why the ignorant remain Weak!||||reg||12
Z||000005||fyerstarter||04-09-2001||11:07 PM||||They don't sell the rims in a 7.5? It's a shame. I think you probably don't want to go over 225 on a 7" rim. Not to mention there is really no need for that wide of tires if you just plan on daily driving anyway.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Eric||04-09-2001||11:52 PM||||The thing about it is, even if you like the look of the wide tires on a narrow rim, people are going to look at the car and say to themselves, "What the hell was he thinking?"  I'm not saying you should do what everyone else thinks you should.  It's just that the little things sometimes detract most from an otherwise pretty package.  ||||reg||1
Z||000007||BAM-BAM||04-10-2001||12:29 AM||||  Eric, I certainly don't want to oversize the tire/wheel ratio. I may have a distorted perception of what I wan't. I appriciate all your inputs. I guess we'll see tomorrow, they are alowing me unlimited access to there tire stock in order to get the right fitment/look. I thought that was pretty cool of Dicount tire. Of course this could be the norm, this is the first set of after market wheels and tires I have ever bought. Thanks again for all the good ideas on what to look for. I promise, I won't over do it. I'll shoot some pics and get some opinions.<P>Peace, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Knowledge is POWER!<BR>That is why the ignorant remain Weak!||||reg||6
Z||000008||Slammed Fiero||04-10-2001||01:06 AM||||I have 225/45/17's pirelli P zero's on the front of my car , fits perfect. handling wise you want the sidewall of the tire to be square with the rim. Right now I have a set of 255/40/17's rear on a 7" rim these were used and all I could afford at the time , they buldge slightly out from the rim , I have since ordered a set of 235/45/17's for the rear , it gives the car the perfect rake and fits square on the rim.<P><BR>JM ||||reg||1
Z||000009||1FST2M6||04-10-2001||10:52 AM||||hehe well i tlooks like other helped ya out..   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P>i was running 215-45s (7") in the front and 235-40s (8") in the rear.. i killed one of the rears and had to get 2 more 215s.. .. well i'm back on 235s now.. anywho..<P>7" wide max tire width should be 235.. thats more than 1" of unsupported tire thats on the wheel.. causing the car to sway at highspeed and have an unpredictable slip angle if you ever do autocross or are in an emergency manuver (child in the road for some strange reason) your tires CAN fold and cause the car to over steer... (in extreme conditions) with a properly supported tire it'll handle the same all the time and won't suprise you in such an evasive manuver. Pirelli P-Zeros are the high dieity of tires. if you can muster up the cash for P-Zeros jump all over them... they are one or 3 tires i've driven on that can be quiet/hold the road to the edge of MY limits not the cars and have a real treadwear rating. P-Zero, SO-2-PP, and AVS Sports. <P>untill i hit the lottery I'm stuck on the Dunlop W10s and had Yokohama A520s. they are decent in the rain, quiet and easy to get if i kill one on the highway or a deepwoods backroad jaunt through the mountains. <P>ok.. take whatever the tire section width is and divide it by 25.4 then subtract the wheel width from that number and divide that by 2. that will give you unsupported sidewall. try to keep it JUST under 1" but over .5 just incase you tag a curb you'll have a little protection. <P>Dunlop W10s 215-45 up front and 235-40 in the rear or Yokohama A520s, Kumho Estra Supra 712s are all decent tires that'll wear ok and handle spirited driving on occasion.<P>hope that helps... <P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig1fst.jpg">     <BR>www.engineered.net<BR>www.epdyno.com<BR>www.enthuzacar.com||||reg||1
